> Well, it is, if you read C really well, since the extra
> parentheses around the assignment, whose value _is_ the
> value of the assignment, are an explicit prod to the C
> compiler to do the needed implicit cast to convert the
> value of the assignment to the compatible type of the
> just enclosing environment; since that is the outer
> parentheses, which are expecting a value of type "bool",
> the implied cast deserves to get you there with no
> warning.

We'd better agree to disagree on this one.  That extra pair of
parentheses, *because* they are extra w.r.t. actual functionality,
makes me think that something else is missing and it takes me
twice as long to read the statement.  I do exactly the same thing
when I see this:

    a = (b * c) + d;

(And before you ask: yes, a page 39 of the original K&R (which
lists C's operator precedence rules) was posted conspicuously in
my cube(s) throughout my <mumble> years of programming for a
living.  After a while, though, it was solely for the benefit of
visitors; it has long since been burned right into my brain.)

Readability comes in many forms; minimal punctuation (and
extremely mininal *extra* punctuation) is one of mine.

